Shared memory types and local implementations/optimizations:

<a> write never / read only

Easy bring it off readonly pages(s)


<b> write once 

initialize to zero page with prot_none. Writer does this

Type* writer = PUBLISH_START(dataptr);

// modify writer

PUBLISH_END(writer);

Behind the scenes this takes dataptr looks up real page it should point to 
map it to a temp page return pointer viz., writer and have it be initialized.
Once done it changes real mapping of initial address to the initialized page
and allows full access (takes off prot_none)

<c> Write Concurrently 
Simple do what you did with your Java implementation. 

Type* local = GETWRITER(original);

// modify local as often as you want **DO NOT PASS AROUND**

FLUSHWRITER(local); // This will take the real lock and merge local writer contents
		    // with main writer contents

<d> Per Thread Lock
    Same as your Distributed Lock in Java except here lock stealing is done by 
    sending the owning thread a signal. 

